{"name":"Little Bear","hex":"#604b42","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Little Bear","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Synonym of Ursa Minor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Little_Bear"}],"entries":[{"title":"Little Bear (Cree)","blocks":[{"text":"Little Bear was a Cree leader who lived in the District of Alberta, Idaho Territory, Montana Territory, and District of Saskatchewan regions of Canada and the United States, in the 19th and early 20th centuries. He is known for his participation in the 1885 North-West Rebellion, which was fought in Alberta and Saskatchewan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Cree_leader_Little_Bear.jpg/330px-Cree_leader_Little_Bear.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Bear_(Cree)","wikidata":"Q6649020"},{"title":"Danny Little Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Danny Little Bear was an American professional wrestler who competed in North American regional promotions including the National Wrestling Alliance, particularly the Central States and Gulf Coast territories, where he remained one of the region's most popular stars during the 1960s and 70s. His feuds with Yasu Fuji & Chati Yokochi, Black Angus Campbell and manager Percival A. Friend and Eduardo Miguel Perez were the cause of frequent riots and arrests in the St. Joseph-Kansas City area during the summer of 1972."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/65/Danny_Little_Bear.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danny_Little_Bear","wikidata":"Q5220561"},{"title":"Little Bear (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Little Bear, also known as Maurice Sendak's Little Bear, is a Canadian children's animated television series co-produced by Nelvana Limited, produced in association with the Canadian Broadcasting Corporation. It is based on the children's book series of the same name written by Else Holmelund Minarik and illustrated by Maurice Sendak. In the United States, the series premiered on Nickelodeon as part of the Nick Jr. block on November 6, 1995, and the final episode aired on June 1, 2001. The series also aired on CBS on Saturday mornings from September 16, 2000, until September 15, 2001."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/03/Little_Bear_title.png/330px-Little_Bear_title.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Bear_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q644013"},{"title":"Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Bears are carnivoran mammals of the family Ursidae. They are classified as caniforms within the suborder Caniformia. Although only eight species of bears are extant, they are widespread, appearing in a wide variety of habitats throughout most of the Northern Hemisphere and partially in the Southern Hemisphere. Bears are found on the continents of North America, South America, and Eurasia. Common characteristics of modern bears include large bodies with stocky legs, long snouts, small rounded ears, shaggy hair, plantigrade paws with five nonretractile claws, and short tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg/330px-Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear","wikidata":"Q11788"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/95/Little_Bear_main_characters.jpg/330px-Little_Bear_main_characters.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/95/Little_Bear_main_characters.jpg/330px-Little_Bear_main_characters.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The main characters","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALittle_Bear_main_characters.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/500px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/1280px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ursidae_distribution.png/500px-Ursidae_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUrsidae_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG/500px-Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG/500px-Plithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plithocyon armagnacensis skull, a member of the extinct subfamily Hemicyoninae from the Miocene","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlithocyon_armagnacensis.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/500px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/1280px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Arctotherium.jpg/500px-Arctotherium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Life restoration of Arctotherium bonariense","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArctotherium.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commo